TITLE: AI-Driven Penetration Testing for ARM Systems: Experimental Evaluation and Deployment Framework Across Four Paradigms
KEYWORDS: ARM architecture; artificial neural networks; cybersecurity; deep learning; edge computing; Internet of Things; large language models; machine learning; penetration testing; reinforcement learning; ARM processors; Benchmarking; Budget control; Cybersecurity; Deep neural networks; Deep reinforcement learning; Edge computing; Learning systems; Memory architecture; Robotic arms; ARM architecture; Cyber security; Deep learning; Edge computing; Language model; Large language model; Machine-learning; Neural-networks; Penetration testing; Reinforcement learnings; Internet of things; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: ARM-based systems are widely deployed in edge and IoT settings, where penetration-Testing tools must operate under strict memory, power, and thermal constraints, yet prior work offers limited deployment-oriented guidance for these platforms. We experimentally evaluated four AI paradigms-traditional machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL), large language models (LLMs), and reinforcement learning (RL)-across three ARM platform tiers (Raspberry Pi 5, Radxa ROCK 5B Plus, NVIDIA Jetson Nano), collecting over 8,000 individual metric observations-aggregating per-iteration latency samples, 100 ms-interval resource readings during sustained-load phases, and 24-hour stability measurements across all paradigms, configurations, and platforms-covering accuracy, memory, latency, power, and thermal behavior. Traditional ML achieved up to 98.1% accuracy with 162 MB peak memory usage, while a combined optimization pipeline for DL (pruning, INT8 quantization-Aware training, distillation, and platform tuning) achieved 92.6% accuracy with a 93% model-size reduction. In contrast, the evaluated LLM configurations exhibited memory and latency requirements that exceeded practical edge budgets on these devices, whereas RL supported lightweight on-device inference via off-device training, using 52-254 MB during execution. We introduce the ARM AI Security Benchmark (AASB) framework to standardize evaluation methodology and provide deployment-oriented baselines for ARM platforms. © 2013 IEEE.

[R0017] year=2025 | venue=Lecture Notes in Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Automated Penetration Testing Through Hierarchical PPO with Large Language Model Enhancement
KEYWORDS: Cyber Security; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Reinforcement Learning; Automation; Computer networks; Deep learning; Deep reinforcement learning; Efficiency; Network security; Action spaces; Computer networks systems; Cyber security; Defense capability; Human expert; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; Reinforcement learnings; Security defense; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is a widely recognized method for assessing the security defense capabilities of computer network systems, traditionally conducted by human experts. Recently, automated penetration testing has emerged as an innovative approach aimed at achieving autonomous penetration testing through the use of reinforcement learning agents. However, this approach encounters significant challenges due to the high complexity of penetration testing scenarios and the inherently low sample efficiency of reinforcement learning methodologies. To address these challenges, we propose a hierarchical deep reinforcement learning model that incorporates a large language model to constrain the action space, enhancing both learning efficiency and overall performance. Firstly, we divided automated penetration testing into two levels of tasks and assigned them to two separate agents, thereby simplifying the action space for each agent. Subsequently, a large language model was introduced to further constrain the action space and leverage the prior knowledge embedded within the model to improve the agent's exploration efficiency. To evaluate the effectiveness and reliability of the proposed approach, we constructed an experimental scenario using scanning information collected from real vulnerable environments. The experimental results demonstrate that agents utilizing our approach can learn optimal strategies more efficiently and exhibit better adaptability in environments with a large number of target hosts. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2025.

[R0018] year=2026 | venue=IEEE International Conference on Intelligence and Security Informatics, ISI | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AGSSA: Agentic Guardian System for SOC Analysts
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Explainable AI; Intrusion Detection; Large Language Models; MITRE ATT&CK; Security Operations Center; SOC Automation; Computation theory; Decision support systems; Feature extraction; Network security; Risk management; Security systems; Telemetering; Telemetering equipment; Agentic AI; Explainable AI; Intrusion-Detection; Language model; Large language model; MITRE ATT&CK; Security operation center; Security operation center automation; Work-flows; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: Security Operations Centers (SOCs) face high alert volumes, fragmented telemetry, and limited analyst capacity. This paper presents AGSSA, an evidence-constrained agentic workflow for explainable SOC decision support. AGSSA combines modality-adaptive detection, feature-level explanation, short- and long-term memory, policy-controlled alert selection, and NIST SP 800-61r3/CSF 2.0-aligned reporting. The LLM is not used as a detector; instead, it converts structured detector outputs, explanation evidence, and memory records into auditable incident reports. AGSSA is evaluated across five datasets covering network-flow, IoT, Sysmon host-process, and heterogeneous host telemetry. On supervised datasets, AGSSA achieves 99.91% accuracy on CIC-IDS2017, 86.98% on UNSW-NB15, and 99.90% on TON_IoT. On MITRE BRAWL, AGSSA reduces 47,026 Sysmon events to 523 review candidates and validates them against BSF red-team ground truth. Results show that AGSSA is best understood not as a new IDS classifier, but as a memoryaware SOC triage workflow that reduces review volume while preserving explanation evidence, ATT&CK context, and report traceability. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0019] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes in Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Short Paper: Evaluating the Capabilities of AI-Based Penetration Testing Tools
KEYWORDS: AI; Assessment Criteria; Penetration Testing; Intelligent agents; Knowledge based systems; Multi agent systems; Academic literature; Assessment criteria; Base agents; Literature reviews; Penetration testing; Penetration testing tools; Performance; Research fields; Technical development; Zero day vulnerabilities; Tools
ABSTRACT: Advances in AI are extending the capabilities of tools for penetration testing. However, due to a fragmented market and rapid technical developments the extent of capabilities and maturity of available tools are not well understood. This short paper provides an overview of this area by reviewing recent academic literature and proposing several assessment criteria. The literature review identifies an active and growing research field, with numerous advancements in the past 18 months. A focus on LLM agents has progressed capabilities towards exploiting zero-day vulnerabilities. Frontier models show superior performance when combined with a focused knowledge base and multi-agent architectures. However, in most cases human involvement is still required, and fully autonomous solutions are not yet evident. To evaluate the maturity of tools, 12 assessment criteria within four broad categories are proposed: AI sophistication, action capabilities, features, and requirements. Maturity levels are proposed for each criterion which enables an objective benchmark of tool capabilities. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.

[R0020] year=2026 | venue=7th International Conference on Computational Vision and Bio Inspired Computing, ICCVBIC 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic Security Validation Framework for Retrieval-Augmented and Tool-Enabled Large Language Model Systems
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; CVSS; EU AI Act; LLM security; MCP security; NIST AI RMF; OWASP LLM Top 10; prompt injection; red-team automation; Formal languages; Guard rails; ISO Standards; Multi agent systems; Network security; Regulatory compliance; Verification; Agentic AI; CVSS; EU AI act; LLM security; Model context protocol security; Model contexts; NIST AI RMF; OWASP LLM top 10; Prompt injection; Protocol security; Red-team automation; Probes
ABSTRACT: The attack surface of an enterprise large language model deployment is no longer a single completion endpoint. Production systems chain LLMs with retrieval databases, Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool servers, and subordinate agents, creating a graph of trust boundaries that static probe-and-detect scanners cannot traverse. This paper introduces ARGUS (Agentic Red-team and Governance Unified Scanner), an opensource framework designed around three observations: attack strategy must adapt to target behavior in real time; payload generation must be structurally novel on every attempt to defeat memorization-based guardrails; and security findings must map directly to regulatory obligations to be actionable. ARGUS replaces the static probe schedule with a closed-loop multi-agent architecture. A Planner agent, backed by a reasoning-capable LLM, formulates and continuously revises the attack plan after each evaluation cycle. An Attacker agent synthesizes payloads subject to an embedding-space diversity constraint that enforces structural novelty across the batch. An Evaluator applies three independent detection layers (semantic proximity scoring, structured LLM-as-judge assessment, and pipeline behavioral trace analysis), eliminating the dependence on keyword signatures that caps detection depth in existing tools. A CVSSv4.0 scoring engine, validated to exact parity with the official FIRST reference implementation across a 48 -vector test battery, assigns versioned severity vectors to every confirmed finding. A compliance mapper links each finding to specific obligations in seven regulatory frameworks: NIST AI RMF, NIST SP 800-228, EU AI Act, US Executive Order 14110, UK AISI evaluation pillars, India CERT-In, and ISO/IEC 42001. Architectural coverage analysis, verified directly against garak v0.15.x source, shows module-level alignment with all ten OWASP LLM Top 10 (2025) categories, compared with full coverage of two categories and partial or no coverage of the remaining eight in garak; a live-target pilot on open-weight models reports real precision, recall, and false-positive rates for two of the ten categories, with full-scale empirical validation left as future work. Native SARIF v2.1 output enables zeroconfiguration integration with GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and Azure DevOps pipelines. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0021] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Data Science and Business Systems, ICDSBS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Automotive Shield for AI Driven Cyber Threat Intelligence
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence in cybersecurity; Automotive cybersecurity; Common vulnerabilities and exposures (CVE); Common vulnerability scoring System (CVSS); Connected and autonomous vehicles (CAVs); Cyber Physical systems (CPS); In-vehicle networks (CAN); Large language models (LLMs); Risk assessment; Threat modeling; Vehicular security; Vulnerability intelligence; Advanced vehicle control systems; Autonomous vehicles; Computer crime; Control systems; Crime; Cyber Physical System; Cybersecurity; Network security; Real time systems; Safety engineering; Artificial intelligence in cybersecurity; Automotive cybersecurity; Automotives; Autonomous Vehicles; Common vulnerabilities and exposures; Common vulnerability and exposure; Common vulnerability scoring system; Common vulnerability scoring systems; Connected and autonomous vehicle; Cybe physical system; Cybe-physical systems; Cyber security; Cyber-physical systems; In-vehicle network (CAN); In-vehicle networks; Language model; Large language model; Risks assessments; Threat modeling; Vehicular security; Vulnerability intelligence; Embedded systems
ABSTRACT: Automobiles have developed into technologically advanced cyber-physical systems, thanks to the use of multiple Electronic Control Unit systems (ECUs) consisting of in-vehicle networking and cloud-based connectivity. These new technologies are intended to automate as well as improve safety and the user experience, but by doing so they have introduced a much larger cyber-attack surface for vehicles. Some examples of these new threats are: CAN Bus Injection, remote exploits against Infotainment Systems, GPS Spoofing, firmware alteration, abuse of over-the-air software updates, all of which directly affect your safety and privacy. Present regulations and industry standards (ISO/SAE 21434) regarding vehicle cyber-attack or generation security do not account for manual procedures associated with an increase in vulnerabilities at an exponential rate. There are no common security tools that can automatically correlate new vulnerabilities to specific vehicle systems or components and determine risk based on context. This paper describes a new framework named Automotive Shield that will provide an AI based approach to assessing vehicle cybersecurity risks in real-time by correlating new vulnerabilities to known vehicle systems or components utilizing a large language model (LLM). Automotive Shield will enable automated reporting of vulnerabilities, real-time scoring of risk associated with each vulnerability, as well as the ability to conduct vehicle cybersecurity assessments at scale for new connected & autonomous vehicles. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0022] year=2026 | venue=Lecture Notes in Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Penetration Testing with AI: Case Studies on LLM and RL-Based Attack Agents
KEYWORDS: Attack Agents; Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; Penetration Testing; Reinforcement Learning; Agents; Computational methods; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Network security; Attack agent; Case-studies; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Language model; Large language model; Network types; Penetration testing; Pressung; Reinforcement learnings; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: The rapid increase in cyber threats and the persistent challenge of limited Cybersecurity resources raise a pressing need for automation and Artificial Intelligence in Penetration Testing. With the range of different network types and configurations associated with dynamic attack sorts and strategies, there is a demand for AI-driven solutions that adapt to various situations. This paper examines two AI-driven approaches: a Large Language Model–based penetration testing agent (PenTest++) and a Reinforcement Learning agent (Cyberwheel), trained in both simulation and emulation scenarios. Our study reveals that the LLM-based agent excels at integrating current threat intelligence and adapting to emerging attack patterns. In contrast, the RL-based agent demonstrates robust performance in controlled, scenario-based environments. The comparative analysis offers valuable insights into the strengths and limitations of each approach, suggesting that a hybrid strategy may yield improved effectiveness in both real-world and controlled settings. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.

[R0023] year=2026 | venue=2026 Intermountain Engineering, Technology and Computing, IETC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Secure-by-Design Framework for Agentic AI: Mitigating Toxic Flows and Adversarial Exploits in Multi-Agent Ecosystems
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Indirect Prompt Injection; Information Flow Control; Model Context Protocol; Secure-by-Design; Toxic Flow Analysis; Behavioral research; Computer programming languages; Data flow analysis; Data transfer; Decision making; Flow control; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network security; Agentic AI; Design frameworks; Flow analysis; Indirect prompt injection; Information flow control; Language model; Model context protocol; Model contexts; Secure-by-design; Toxic flow analyze; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: Large Language Model (LLM)-driven autonomous agents leveraging the Model Context Protocol (MCP) for tool interaction face a critical vulnerability class we term "Toxic Flows."These attacks propagate malicious instructions through untrusted data sources to compromise agent decision-making, enabling unauthorized actions and data exfiltration. We present a Secure-by-Design (SbD) framework centered on Toxic Flow Analysis (TFA), adapting source-to-sink reachability concepts to agentic workflows where non-deterministic LLM behavior precludes traditional dataflow assumptions. Our formal model employs a product lattice decomposing tool capabilities into orthogonal confidentiality, integrity, and side-effect dimensions, with explicit soundness properties under stated assumptions. We formalize trust propagation through information-flow lattices, introduce verifiable sanitizer specifications for constrained output domains, and handle cyclic agent loops through monotonic fixed-point iteration over the full lattice structure. We implement TFA as a Python framework and evaluate it on a synthetic benchmark (500 graphs, seed=42). Results demonstrate 94.2% attack detection with 3.1% false positive rate and 47ms average latency. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0024] year=2026 | venue=Cluster Computing | type=Article
TITLE: Explainable AI and few-shot prompting in an LLM-based agent for network intrusion detection systems
KEYWORDS: Convolutional Neural Networks; Cybersecurity; Explainable AI; Large Language Models; LIME; Network Intrusion Detection; Computational linguistics; Computer crime; Convolutional neural networks; Deep learning; Intrusion detection; Learning systems; Natural language processing systems; Network intrusion; Network security; Convolutional neural network; Cyber security; Explainable AI; Language model; Large language model; Local interpretable model-agnostic explanation; Model agents; Model-based OPC; Network intrusion detection; Network intrusion detection systems; Convolution
ABSTRACT: Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are essential for defending against increasingly sophisticated cyber threats, yet many deep learning approaches remain difficult to interpret and depend on fixed attack labels. This paper presents a multi-stage framework that combines a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for binary traffic classification, Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) for instance-level explanations, and a large language model (LLM)-based agent for analyst-facing interpretation. The CNN is trained only for benign-versus-attack discrimination, thereby avoiding per-attack-type supervision at the detector stage, after which LIME highlights the most influential features for each decision. An LLM agent then converts these feature attributions and the CNN’s binary prediction into concise natural-language justifications and, when the evidence is sufficiently distinctive, proposes a likely attack family for analyst review. Experiments on the CSE-CIC-IDS2018 benchmark show that the CNN attains 94.94% accuracy and a 94.69% F1-score on the balanced test split used in this study, while the LLM agent reaches 97% agreement with ground-truth labels (95% Wilson confidence interval: 91.5–99.0%). The results indicate that coupling local XAI with LLM-based interpretation can improve the readability and auditability of NIDS outputs. The current framework is intended as a post-hoc analyst-support layer rather than a strict real-time inline defense, and broader validation under operational traffic distributions remains future work. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.

[R0025] year=2025 | venue=Hunan Daxue Xuebao/Journal of Hunan University Natural Sciences | type=Article
TITLE: Generative AI–Guided Sentinel for Self-Optimizing Federated Cybersecurity and Intelligent Threat Detection; [生成式人工智能引导的哨兵系统，用于自我优化的联邦网络安全与智能威胁检测]
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Federated Learning; Generative AI; Intrusion Detection; Large Language Model; 联邦学习、大语言模型、网络安全、入侵检测、生成式人工智能
ABSTRACT: As cyber threats become increasingly sophisticated and pervasive, adaptive, intelligent, and privacy-preserving intrusion detection systems (IDSs) are more critical than ever, particularly in ecosystem-based networks. However, existing federated cybersecurity systems continue to face several challenges, including reduced data processing efficiency caused by data heterogeneity, difficulties in real-time threat detection, and complex configuration management. To address these challenges, we propose a novel Self-Improving Federated Cybersecurity Sentinel framework that integrates Federated Learning (FL) with Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) to enable dynamic and context-aware optimization. Large Language Models (LLMs) play a central role in the proposed framework by enabling prompt-driven analytics for real-time intrusion detection, feature relevance assessment, automated anomaly investigation, and adaptive optimization of FL parameters. Experimental evaluations conducted on the UNSW-NB15 dataset demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves a precision of 0.96 and an F1-score of 0.98, while simultaneously reducing configuration adjustment time by approximately 68%. These results indicate that the framework significantly simplifies the tuning process and enhances detection performance. Overall, this study represents a substantial advancement toward fully autonomous and robust cybersecurity systems. Future work will focus on improving the generalizability of the framework across diverse threat scenarios and incorporating explainable AI components to enhance transparency and interpretability. Copyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee JHU

[R0026] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 2026 IEEE 3rd International Conference on Big Data Science and Engineering, ICBDSE 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Ai SecureDataGuard: A Big Data Security, Privacy-Preserving and Visualization System for LLM-Powered Smart Homes
KEYWORDS: Anomaly Detection; Big Data Security; Data Privacy; Data Visualization; Differential Privacy; Federated Learning; LLM Security; Multi-Agent Collaboration; Smart Home; Anomaly detection; Automation; Big data; Data visualization; Differential privacy; Information leakage; Intelligent agents; Intelligent buildings; Internet of things; Network security; Privacy-preserving techniques; Security systems; Smart homes; Visualization; Agent collaboration; Anomaly detection; Big data security; Differential privacies; LLM security; Multi agent; Multi-agent collaboration; Privacy preserving; Security/privacy; Smart homes; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Now large language models and that multi-agent frameworks are widely used, platforms like MOSS can let users talk to smart homes naturally and link up all kinds of household devices. But there's a slew of tough issues hindering these products from practical commercial application IoT data from every device, constant user privacy leaks, intentional prompt injection and safety guardrail evasion attacks, plus no tool to monitor all risks instantly.For the resolution of all aforementioned drawbacks, I built a complete big data security framework named SecureDataGuard. It packs malicious input screening, differential privacy, federated lightweight anomaly tracking and real-time data visual monitoring into one set of tools. I built the whole system on top of MOSS AI's multi-agent structure, and also adopted mature industrial AI security tools from Anquan Data Intelligence.First, I made a two-step intent recognition algorithm to block prompt injection and jailbreak attempts. Second, I put together a mixed privacy protection scheme, so we can use data normally without exposing raw user information. I also developed a slimmed-down Transformer model called LADT to catch abnormal device behaviors the second they happen. On top of that, I built a monitoring panel with StarRocks and ECharts, so administrators can track every risk across bird's-eye view of the whole system.A large set of empirical experiments was implemented with real-world smart home operation records to corroborate the system's performance.. Encouraging results are observed in our experiments. The system accurately recognizes user commands with an accuracy rate of 97.8%.Identifies anomalous activity within 18 ms and lowers privacy leakage probability by 92%. All 18 key security metrics on the dashboard update within milliseconds. This work delivers a practical, deployable technical solution to handle smart home data safety, privacy risks, Anomalous Event Detection and Visual Risk Control © 2026 IEEE.

[R0027] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Data Science for Cyber-Physical Systems Resilience using Advanced Applications, ICDCA 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Cognitive Architecture for Offensive Cyberspace Operations: A Recursive Large Language Model Framework
KEYWORDS: Automated Penetration Testing; Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; OODA Loop; Recursive Cognitive Loop; Shadow Graph; Automation; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Automated penetration testing; Cognitive architectures; Cyber security; Cyberspaces; Language model; Large language model; Observe orient decide act' loop; Penetration testing; Recursive cognitive loop; Shadow graph; Network security
ABSTRACT: The present day cybersecurity environment is marked by an ever-increasing asymmetry between offense and defense. As software systems become increasingly complex, so too is the 'window of exposure' for critical vulnerabilities. This is particularly true to the point that human penetration testing is no longer sufficient to keep pace with system complexity. Conventional approaches to Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing (VAPT) are limited by the scalability and determinism of conventional automated scanners. This research proposes the design and development of an Autonomous Offensive Cyber Security Framework that incorporates Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate all aspects of penetration testing. We present here a novel concept for a Recursive Cognitive Loop (RCL) that incorporates an LLM to serve as an orchestrator that replicates the 'Observe Orient Decide Act' (OODA) loop of human thought. By leveraging a Shadow Graph for state management and Retrieval-Augmented Generation for real-time exploit retrieval, this framework is shown to be capable of autonomously narrowing the scope of attacks from broad segments of the network to specific parameter-level exploits. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0028] year=2025 | venue=IEEE International Conference on Data Mining Workshops, ICDMW | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AutoBnB-RAG: Enhancing Multi-Agent Incident Response with Retrieval-Augmented Generation
KEYWORDS: cybersecurity; incident response; large language models; multiagent systems; retrieval-augmented generation; Autonomous agents; Behavioral research; Computation theory; Computational methods; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Distributed computer systems; Information retrieval; Intelligent agents; Network security; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Decisions makings; External knowledge; Incident response; Informed decision; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Retrieval-augmented generation; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Incident response (IR) requires fast, coordinated, and well-informed decision-making to contain and mitigate cyber threats. While large language models (LLMs) have shown promise as autonomous agents in simulated IR settings, their reasoning is often limited by a lack of access to external knowledge. In this work, we present AutoBnB-RAG, an extension of the AutoBnB framework that incorporates retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) into multi-agent incident response simulations. Built on the Backdoors & Breaches (B&B) tabletop game environment, AutoBnB-RAG enables agents to issue retrieval queries and incorporate external evidence during collaborative investigations. We introduce two retrieval settings: one grounded in curated technical documentation (RAG-Wiki), and another using narrative-style incident reports (RAG-News). We evaluate performance across eight team structures, including newly introduced argumentative configurations designed to promote critical reasoning. To validate practical utility, we also simulate real-world cyber incidents based on public breach reports, demonstrating AutoBnB-RAG's ability to reconstruct complex multi-stage attacks. Our results show that retrieval augmentation improves decision quality and success rates across diverse organizational models. This work demonstrates the value of integrating retrieval mechanisms into LLM-based multi-agent systems for cybersecurity decision-making. © 2025 IEEE.


